======================================================================
  THE n -> n-2 RECURSIVE STRUCTURE
======================================================================

  Ambiguous score classes (H varies within score):
  n=4: 0 ambiguous classes (out of 4)
  n=5: 3 ambiguous classes (out of 12)
  n=6: 41 ambiguous classes (out of 56)

  THE MAPPING: If we restrict G_n to the most ambiguous score class,
  does it look like G_{n-2}?

  G_3: 2 vertices, 1 edge
  G_4: 4 vertices, 5 edges. Ambiguous at score (1,1,2,2): maps to G_2 (trivial)
  G_5: 12 vertices, 30 edges. Ambiguous at score (1,2,2,2,3): 3 classes -> maps to G_3 (2 classes)?
  G_6: 56 vertices, 290 edges. Most ambiguous score: maps to G_4?

  COMPARISON:
    n=5 PoS class: 3 iso classes, H in {11,13,15}
    G_3:           2 iso classes, H in {1,3}
    Ratio: 3/2 = 1.5

    n=6 PoS class (1,2,2,3,3,4): ~12 iso classes, H in {23,25,29,31,33,37}
    G_4:           4 iso classes, H in {1,3,5}
    Ratio: ~12/4 = 3.0

  The PoS class has MORE iso classes than G_{n-2}.
  G_{n-2} embeds as a COARSENING, not an isomorphism.
  The extra classes come from the INTERACTION between
  the removed source/sink and the middle tournament.

======================================================================
  SELF-LOOP ANALYSIS (intra-class flips)
======================================================================

  n=3: total_flips=24, self=12 (50.0%), inter=12 (50.0%)
  n=4: total_flips=384, self=144 (37.5%), inter=240 (62.5%)
  n=5: total_flips=10240, self=1760 (17.2%), inter=8480 (82.8%)
  n=6: total_flips=491520, self=37920 (7.7%), inter=453600 (92.3%)

  Self-loop fraction = fraction of arc flips that stay in the same iso class.
  This INCREASES with n because larger classes have more internal structure.
  It's the 'label noise' fraction from the unlabeling analysis.

